// F109: Hexagonal sampling kept its textbook saving on synthetic fields; three forecasts about why failed
// The BuildTeX source of this whitepaper. Built with: buildc run, then python -m buildtex.
mod doc;
use doc::*;

fn main() ~ Console + FileSystem {
    let mut d = doc_new("Hexagonal sampling kept its textbook saving on synthetic fields; three forecasts about why failed");
    theme(&mut d, "paper");
    meta(&mut d, "pdf_author", "Zain Dana Harper");
    meta(&mut d, "subject", "A preregistered test of hexagonal, body-centred cubic and geodesic sampling lattices and of first-order fields inside cells on synthetic two-dimensional, three-dimensional, spherical and time-frequency signals");
    meta(&mut d, "footer", "F109, hexagonal lattices across media on synthetic signals, version 1.0");
    blk(&mut d, "title", "Hexagonal sampling kept its textbook saving on synthetic fields; three forecasts about why failed");
    blk(&mut d, "subtitle", "A preregistered comparison of hexagonal and related lattices against square ones in the plane, in three dimensions, on the sphere and in time-frequency, on synthetic signals, with jittered-lattice controls");
    blk(&mut d, "author", "Zain Dana Harper");
    blk(&mut d, "meta", "Version 1.0, 14 November 2026. Finding F109 in the findings ledger. Tier B. Synthetic arms only.");
    blk(&mut d, "abstracthead", "Abstract");
    blk(&mut d, "abstract", "A hexagonal grid covers the plane more evenly than a square one, and sampling theory says it needs fewer samples for the same signal. We asked whether that idea carries beyond the plane, to volumes, the sphere and sound, and why it works where it does. Three forecasts failed. In three dimensions the forecast that the densest packing lattice samples best failed: the body-centred cubic lattice needed 0.707 of the cubic sample count at the strict limit against 0.770 for the face-centred lattice. On the sphere a geodesic grid had a tenth of the error of a latitude-longitude grid at 1,000 samples (1.00 log10 units, 95% interval 0.99 to 1.02), but a jittered geodesic grid reached 0.83 on average and 1.10 at its 95th percentile, so the test failed its control. The forecast that the sphere gain would shrink for fields aligned with the grid axis also failed. On 30 synthetic test images at 4,096 samples, hexagonal sampling scored 0.43 dB below square sampling (interval -0.85 to -0.12). The textbook results held as instrument checks. In the plane the hexagonal lattice needed 0.866 of the square count for strictly band-limited fields and 0.905 to 0.922 at matched error for a Gaussian spectrum. A first-order field inside each cell beat a constant cell at equal parameter count in every draw: by 0.26 log10 units in three dimensions and 0.54 on the sphere. The study's tests on real images and a voice recording used private material and are left out; all seven image verdicts failed.");
    labelled(&mut d, "section", "sec:reader", "What this means for a reader");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "The hexagon's advantage is a statement about ideal signals with no edges, and it is smaller in practice than the famous 13.4 percent: 8 to 9 percent for a smooth spectrum at matched error. On a sphere most of the gain over a latitude-longitude grid comes from covering the surface evenly, and our test could not credit the regular hexagonal arrangement beyond that. In a rectangular image at equal sample count, the hexagonal grid lost on our synthetic set. Anyone choosing a grid should test it on bounded data of the kind they will store.");
    labelled(&mut d, "section", "sec:intro", "Introduction");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "Petersen and Middleton showed in 1962 that a signal whose spectrum is confined to a disc can be sampled on a hexagonal lattice with 13.4 percent fewer samples than on a square one {cite:petersen1962}. The same argument in three dimensions favours the body-centred cubic lattice, because the best sampling lattice is the one whose reciprocal lattice packs spheres most densely {cite:entezari}. A later analysis reports that this holds near the strict sampling limit and that another lattice can win at low sampling rates {cite:agrell}. In time-frequency analysis, Strohmer and Beaver conjectured that the hexagonal lattice gives the best-conditioned Gabor frame for a Gaussian window, with a condition number near 1.26 at redundancy 2 against the square lattice's 1.41 {cite:strohmer2003,faulhuber2019}. We know these sources through search summaries and abstracts.");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "The study began from a 2026 preprint on rendering scenes with soft hexagonal patches that each carry a first-order colour field {cite:kaushik2026}. That design has two ingredients, a hexagonal layout and a field that varies inside each cell. The question was whether either ingredient pays in other media, and the decision it informed was which grid and which kind of cell our own tools use to store sampled images, volumes, spherical fields and sound.");
    labelled(&mut d, "section", "sec:prereg", "Preregistration and design");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "The preregistration was hash-locked on 30 October 2026 (UTC), before the full run. It was not blind. Small trial runs of every part had been seen, and the preregistration lists what they showed and what was changed after them. The forecast for fast sweeps in sound was written after a trial run had shown the direction.");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "The study scored 21 verdicts: 10 passed and 11 failed. This paper reports the 11 that rest on synthetic signals alone, of which 8 passed and 3 failed. It leaves out the rest:");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "Seven verdicts on images were scored on a pool of 60 images, half of them one person's saved pictures. That material is private. All seven failed on the pool, including the main forecast that hexagonal sampling would beat square sampling. We report the synthetic half only, as a subset that was not the preregistered test.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "Two verdicts on sound pooled synthetic clips with a recording of a voice, which is also private. One passed and one failed.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "One exploratory verdict on musical intervals used a consonance ranking entered from memory and should not be cited.");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "A first full run with the same seed was stopped by the run harness's tamper guard when two compiled-code cache files appeared in the library folder during the run. Their origin is unknown. The run was repeated unchanged, and all 1,454 numeric values in the two result files agree.");
    labelled(&mut d, "section", "sec:data", "Data and provenance");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "Every signal in this paper was generated by the study's code from a fixed seed: random fields with a Gaussian isotropic spectrum in two and three dimensions, random spherical-harmonic fields to degree 40, 30 synthetic test images (isotropic noise at two slopes, random discs, axis-aligned blocks, grids and text, the same rotated by 30 and 45 degrees, smooth gradients, and gradients cut by straight edges), and three kinds of synthetic sound (a slow chirp, chords with vibrato and glides, and fast frequency sweeps). No external dataset is used.");
    labelled(&mut d, "section", "sec:methods", "Methods");
    blk(&mut d, "subsection", "Sampling in the plane and in three dimensions");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "A field with power {m:\\exp(-k^2 / 2)} at spatial frequency {m:k} was sampled on a lattice and rebuilt with that lattice's ideal low-pass filter; the error is exact by Parseval's theorem. The strict test used a flat disc (or ball) spectrum and found the lowest density with no aliasing. For the plane, theory gives the ratio of hexagonal to square density as");
    labelled(&mut d, "eq", "eq:hex", "\\frac{\\rho_{hex} }{\\rho_{sq} } = \\frac{\\sqrt{3} }{2}");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "which is 0.866, and for the body-centred cubic lattice against the cubic lattice {m:1 / \\sqrt{2} }, which is 0.707. The matched-error test found the density at which the expected relative error crosses 0.01, 0.001 and 0.0001, by Monte Carlo over one million frequencies. The paired advantage of lattice {m:b} over lattice {m:a} on one field is");
    labelled(&mut d, "eq", "eq:adv", "D = \\log_{10} \\frac{e_a}{e_b}");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "with {m:e} the relative squared error. In the plane it was measured at a density of 36 samples per unit area on 200 fields.");
    blk(&mut d, "subsection", "The sphere");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "Fields were sampled at the points of a geodesic grid, of a latitude-longitude grid sampled at cell centres, and of a Fibonacci spiral, and rebuilt by least squares in spherical harmonics. Errors were interpolated to 1,000 samples on 40 fields. Isotropic fields spread power evenly over orders; zonal fields vary with latitude only.");
    blk(&mut d, "subsection", "Images");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "Each 256-pixel grey image was smoothed, sampled at exactly 4,096 points on a square or a hexagonal lattice, and rebuilt with a Gaussian kernel. The two kernel widths were tuned per lattice on 8 held-out tuning images, 4 of them private, then frozen. The score is the difference in PSNR over all pixels.");
    blk(&mut d, "subsection", "Cells with a field inside");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "A volume or a spherical field was stored as cells at a budget of 1,500 numbers: one number per constant cell, or a value and its gradient per first-order cell (4 numbers in three dimensions, 3 on the sphere). The field effect is eq. {ref:eq:adv} for first-order against constant cells, and the grid effect is the same for body-centred against cubic cells or geodesic against latitude-longitude cells, on 6 random smooth fields.");
    blk(&mut d, "subsection", "Time-frequency lattices");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "For a signal of 960 samples, a rectangular lattice of time-frequency points was compared with a hexagonal one at the same number of points. The frame condition number {m:B / A} of the Gabor frame was computed for the best of 41 Gaussian window widths at five redundancies. Sound segments were then approximated by 120 numbers with a greedy sparse solver, using atoms of fixed frequency (3 numbers each) or chirplets whose frequency changes linearly inside the atom (4 numbers).");
    blk(&mut d, "subsection", "Intervals and correction");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "Each 95% interval comes from 2,000 bootstrap resamples over fields, images or segments. One-sided p values were corrected with the Benjamini-Hochberg correction {cite:bh1995} over the study's 24 tests, which include the tests left out here.");
    labelled(&mut d, "section", "sec:controls", "Controls and planted checks");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "The control for a lattice claim is the same lattice with each point moved at random by 0.15 of a spacing. A jittered lattice keeps the even coverage and loses the regular arrangement. A lattice result passes only if the regular lattice's advantage exceeds the 95th percentile of the jittered lattice's advantage over the same reference, across 40 control draws.");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "The strict density ratios serve as the planted check: they are known from geometry, and the code had to reproduce them without being given them. Those values, and the frame condition numbers, are deterministic. They could not have come out otherwise if the code is right, so they test the instrument and are not findings.");
    labelled(&mut d, "section", "sec:results", "Results");
    blk(&mut d, "subsection", "What failed");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "In three dimensions the forecast that the densest packing lattice samples best failed: the body-centred cubic lattice needed 0.707 of the cubic sample count at the strict limit against 0.770 for the face-centred lattice. The counter-forecast, that the winner is the lattice whose reciprocal packs densest, passed. Table {ref:tab:counts} gives the counts. At matched error the body-centred lattice also needed fewer samples than the face-centred one, but only by 1 percent at an error of 0.01 and 0.3 percent at 0.001, and the result file holds no interval for these counts. We do not claim a difference between those two lattices away from the strict limit.");
    labelled(&mut d, "tablecap", "tab:counts", "Samples per unit area or volume needed by each lattice, with the ratio to the square or cubic lattice. Strict: no aliasing of a disc or ball spectrum. Matched: Gaussian spectrum at the stated relative error.");
    attributed(&mut d, "thead", "", "Lattice¦Strict¦Error 0.01¦Error 0.001¦Error 0.0001", "cols=0.28,0.18,0.18,0.18,0.18");
    blk(&mut d, "row", "Square¦4.00¦36.6¦53.7¦70.3");
    blk(&mut d, "row", "Hexagonal¦3.46 (0.866)¦33.7 (0.922)¦48.7 (0.905)¦64.5 (0.918)");
    blk(&mut d, "row", "Cubic¦8.00¦248.7¦421.1¦not run");
    blk(&mut d, "row", "Body-centred cubic¦5.66 (0.707)¦205.4 (0.826)¦343.9 (0.817)¦not run");
    attributed(&mut d, "row", "", "Face-centred cubic¦6.16 (0.770)¦207.3 (0.834)¦344.8 (0.819)¦not run", "last=1");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "On the sphere a geodesic grid had a tenth of the error of a latitude-longitude grid at 1,000 samples (1.00 log10 units, 95% interval 0.99 to 1.02), but a jittered geodesic grid reached 0.83 on average and 1.10 at its 95th percentile, so the test failed its control. Figure {ref:fig:controls} shows the plane and the sphere side by side. The regular geodesic grid is above the jittered grid's average and inside its spread. To reach a relative error of 0.01 the geodesic grid needed 680 samples, the Fibonacci spiral 742 and the latitude-longitude grid 1,124. The geodesic grid's error was below the Fibonacci spiral's by 0.24 log10 units (0.22 to 0.25). The reading that fits these numbers is that even coverage carries the gain over latitude and longitude, and that the test could not show the hexagonal arrangement adds to it.");
    attributed(&mut d, "figure", "fig:controls", "Advantage of the regular lattice over the reference grid (diamond, with 95% interval) against the same advantage for a jittered copy of the lattice (grey band from the 5th to the 95th percentile, tick at the mean). Plane: hexagonal against square. Sphere: geodesic against latitude-longitude. Drawn from the run's result file.", "src=fig-controls.png;width=0.8;alt=Two rows. In the plane the hexagonal advantage of 0.18 sits well to the right of the jittered control band. On the sphere the geodesic advantage of 1.00 sits inside the jittered control band, which runs from 0.68 to 1.10");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "The third failure is a transfer forecast. If the hexagon's gain came from treating all directions alike, the geodesic advantage should be smaller for zonal fields, which line up with the latitude-longitude grid. It was not: the advantage was 1.08 for zonal fields against 1.00 for isotropic ones, a difference of -0.08 (-0.18 to +0.03) in the direction opposite to the forecast.");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "On 30 synthetic test images at 4,096 samples, hexagonal sampling scored 0.43 dB below square sampling (interval -0.85 to -0.12). This is a subset of the preregistered pool and so carries no verdict. A check made after the run scored only pixels two sample spacings or more from the image edge; there the difference on the same 30 images was +0.09 dB (-0.13 to +0.39). That fits a loss at the border, where a square lattice tiles a rectangle exactly and a hexagonal one leaves ragged margins, but the check was chosen after the result was seen and its interval includes zero.");
    blk(&mut d, "subsection", "What held");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "In the plane the hexagonal lattice needed 0.866 of the square count for strictly band-limited fields and 0.905 to 0.922 at matched error for a Gaussian spectrum. The saving at matched error is 8 to 9 percent. The hexagonal lattice's expected error was lower at every density tested, and at a density of 36 its advantage was 0.178 log10 units (0.174 to 0.181, 200 fields) against a jittered-lattice 95th percentile of 0.031.");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "Hexagonal time-frequency lattices had the lower frame condition number at all five redundancies (Table {ref:tab:cond}). Our value at redundancy 2 is 1.262, close to the 1.26 reported in the literature for the continuous case.");
    labelled(&mut d, "tablecap", "tab:cond", "Frame condition number B/A for the best Gaussian window, 960-sample periodic signals. Lower is better; 1 is ideal.");
    attributed(&mut d, "thead", "", "Redundancy¦Rectangular¦Hexagonal", "cols=0.34,0.33,0.33");
    blk(&mut d, "row", "1.25¦2.385¦2.160");
    blk(&mut d, "row", "1.5¦1.732¦1.590");
    blk(&mut d, "row", "2¦1.414¦1.262");
    blk(&mut d, "row", "3¦1.075¦1.041");
    attributed(&mut d, "row", "", "4¦1.015¦1.007", "last=1");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "A first-order field inside each cell beat a constant cell at equal parameter count in every draw: by 0.26 log10 units in three dimensions and 0.54 on the sphere. The grid effect at the same budget was smaller: 0.04 for body-centred against cubic cells and 0.18 for geodesic against latitude-longitude cells, also with the same sign in all 6 fields. Figure {ref:fig:cells} shows every draw.");
    attributed(&mut d, "figure", "fig:cells", "Field effect (first-order against constant cells) and grid effect (body-centred against cubic, geodesic against latitude-longitude) at 1,500 parameters, one dot per random field, in log10 units of error. Drawn from the run's result file.", "src=fig-cells.png;width=0.74;alt=Four rows of six dots each. Field effects cluster near 0.26 in three dimensions and 0.54 on the sphere, grid effects cluster near 0.04 and 0.18");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "In sound, the first-order idea paid only where the frequency moves fast. On fast sweeps chirplets beat fixed atoms by 1.86 dB (0.89 to 2.76, 16 segments), which passed. On the slow chirp they lost 2.53 dB (-2.83 to -2.23) and on synthetic chords 0.68 dB (-0.80 to -0.56). The gain rose with sweep rate at a rank correlation of 0.52 (-0.01 to 0.77), which did not survive correction (corrected p 0.054). On six smooth synthetic images a first-order colour field gained 0.38 dB (0.08 to 0.72) at equal parameter count; this is a subset with no verdict.");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "With fixed atoms, the hexagonal time-frequency lattice beat the rectangular one on each synthetic clip: by 3.29 dB on the slow chirp, 0.72 dB on chords and 1.03 dB on fast sweeps. This comparison mixes two things. At redundancy 2 the hexagonal lattice's best window is 1.8 times wider than the rectangular lattice's (20.6 against 11.3 samples), so the gain cannot be assigned to the lattice alone.");
    labelled(&mut d, "section", "sec:discussion", "Discussion");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "The forecasts that failed share one assumption: that the hexagon wins because it is the most even or most isotropic arrangement, and that this reason travels. In three dimensions the lattice with the best packing lost to the lattice whose reciprocal has the best packing, which is the textbook result and which our literal forecast had ignored. On the sphere, isotropy did not predict where the gain was largest, and jittering the grid did not remove the gain. On bounded images the lattice's interior efficiency was outweighed by something else, plausibly the border.");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "The ingredient that did transfer is the first-order field, and only where the signal is smooth across a cell: volumes, spherical fields, smooth images and fast sweeps. It lost on slowly changing sound, where a fixed atom already fits.");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "For our own tools the result set these rules: store volumes on the body-centred lattice and spherical fields on a geodesic or Fibonacci grid, keep the square grid for bounded images unless a fresh test with padded or periodic borders says otherwise, and give cells a first-order field where the signal is smooth at the cell scale.");
    labelled(&mut d, "section", "sec:limits", "Limitations");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "Everything here is synthetic. The fields have Gaussian isotropic spectra, and the synthetic images are constructed patterns. Results on natural images, recorded sound or measured volumes may differ.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "The image result is a subset of a preregistered pool whose other half is private, so it carries no verdict. The kernel widths were tuned on 8 images that included 4 private ones.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "The border explanation for the image loss was formed after the result was seen, and its supporting check has an interval that includes zero.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "The sphere test used one jitter size (0.15 of a spacing) and 40 control draws. A smaller advantage of the regular arrangement over jitter is not excluded; the averages differ by 0.17 log10 units.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "Matched-error sample counts have no intervals in the result file. The body-centred and face-centred lattices differ there by 1 percent or less.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "The cell tests used 6 fields and one budget.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "The lattice gain in sparse coding of sound is confounded with window width.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "The strict ratios and the frame condition numbers are deterministic checks of the code and add nothing new to the literature.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "The comparison says nothing about hexagonal patches whose positions and shapes are optimised, as in the preprint that prompted the study; its claims were not tested here.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "The study was not blind: trial runs were seen before the lock. Scoring was by our own code.");
    labelled(&mut d, "section", "sec:verified", "Verified");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "Verified: on 14 November 2026 the strict densities and matched counts in the plane and in three dimensions, the plane advantage with its interval and control, the sphere advantages, intervals, sample counts and jitter control, the zonal difference, the synthetic image difference and its interval, the five pairs of frame condition numbers and window widths, the cell effects and all 24 per-field values, the per-clip sound effects and intervals, the corrected p values and the 21 verdicts with their rules were re-read from the run's result file. They match the findings ledger. The interior image check was re-read from the post hoc file. The preregistration's SHA-256 matches the hash in the result file and in the lock file. The ratios in Table 1 at matched error for three dimensions were computed for this paper from the counts in the result file. All three figures were drawn by a script that reads only the result file. The study was not re-run for this paper, and the agreement of the blocked first run with the second was taken from the ledger and not recounted. The literature was found by two searches run on 14 November 2026 and read at the level of search snippets and abstracts; the full texts were not read. The preprint on hexagonal patches is cited as the preregistration records it and was not read for this paper. The Benjamini and Hochberg paper is cited for the method only.");
    labelled(&mut d, "section", "sec:corrections", "Corrections");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "14 November 2026: narrowed to the synthetic arms. The ledger entry's image results pool 30 synthetic images with 30 private ones, and its sound results include a private voice recording; those pooled values, a comparison with a preprint's image claims that used the same pool, and an exploratory test on musical intervals are left out. The ledger's statement that hexagonal sampling loses 0.21 dB on images is replaced here by the synthetic subset, -0.43 dB on 30 images.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "14 November 2026: the ledger says the body-centred lattice \"wins every test\". This paper states that its margin over the face-centred lattice at matched error is 1 percent or less with no interval, and limits the claim to the strict limit.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "14 November 2026: first published version.");
    labelled(&mut d, "section", "sec:repro", "Reproduction");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "Data: none external. Every signal is synthetic and generated by the study's code from the seed 2026102961; nothing is licensed from a third party.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "Plane and three dimensions: sample fields with power {m:\\exp(-k^2 / 2)} on each lattice, rebuild with the ideal low-pass of the lattice, and find the strict and matched densities; check eq. {ref:eq:hex}.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "Sphere: sample spherical-harmonic fields to degree 40 on geodesic, latitude-longitude, Fibonacci and jittered geodesic grids, rebuild by least squares, and compute eq. {ref:eq:adv} at 1,000 samples.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "Images: sample each synthetic image at 4,096 points on square and hexagonal lattices with tuned Gaussian prefilter and rebuild kernel, and take the PSNR difference.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "Cells and sound: as in the Methods, at 1,500 and 120 parameters.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "Preregistration SHA-256: 9096a776ae17fca5796129d41ea87482afbf78134ca658cd3e3917ae56cd9014. Plan SHA-256: d0d9b88ba4aabac9f6bcd0fde4559e09f2cd01e77b427a4e143bc19c2a9920e0. Run 20261030T073040Z.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "Cost: the analysis took 8,854 s on one desktop computer, including the parts left out here.");
    blk(&mut d, "pagebreak", "");
    labelled(&mut d, "section", "sec:appendix", "Appendix A. The preregistered verdicts");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "Verdicts as scored by the run. \"Synthetic\" marks the verdicts this paper stands on. \"Pooled\" marks verdicts scored on data that included private material; their values are not reported.");
    labelled(&mut d, "tablecap", "tab:verdicts", "All 21 verdicts of the study.");
    attributed(&mut d, "thead", "", "Verdict¦What it asked¦Data¦Outcome", "cols=0.30,0.40,0.15,0.15");
    blk(&mut d, "row", "Plane instrument¦strict ratio, matched ratio, advantage above jitter¦synthetic¦passed");
    blk(&mut d, "row", "Hexagonal beats square on images¦mean PSNR difference above 0¦pooled¦failed");
    blk(&mut d, "row", "Isotropy predicts the image gain¦negative rank correlation¦pooled¦failed");
    blk(&mut d, "row", "Rotation test on images¦rotated lattice keeps and changes as forecast¦pooled¦failed");
    blk(&mut d, "row", "Hexagonal disc layout¦beats square layout after correction¦pooled¦failed");
    blk(&mut d, "row", "Adaptive discs¦beat both fixed layouts¦pooled¦failed");
    blk(&mut d, "row", "Hexagon shape¦beats Gaussian shape at equal parameters¦pooled¦failed");
    blk(&mut d, "row", "Direction of a preprint's image claim¦both directions agree¦pooled¦failed");
    blk(&mut d, "row", "Frame condition¦hexagonal below rectangular at every redundancy¦synthetic¦passed");
    blk(&mut d, "row", "Rebuild under noise¦hexagonal above rectangular¦pooled¦passed");
    blk(&mut d, "row", "Chirplets on fast sweeps¦field effect above 0¦synthetic¦passed");
    blk(&mut d, "row", "Isotropy in time-frequency¦negative rank correlation¦pooled¦failed");
    blk(&mut d, "row", "Pitch geometry (exploratory)¦ranking from memory¦not cited¦passed");
    blk(&mut d, "row", "Densest packing wins in 3-D¦face-centred needs fewest samples¦synthetic¦failed");
    blk(&mut d, "row", "Reciprocal packing wins in 3-D¦body-centred needs fewest samples¦synthetic¦passed");
    blk(&mut d, "row", "Geodesic sphere grid¦advantage above 0 and above jitter's 95th percentile¦synthetic¦failed");
    blk(&mut d, "row", "Zonal fields on the sphere¦smaller geodesic advantage¦synthetic¦failed");
    blk(&mut d, "row", "Field in 3-D cells¦first-order better in every field¦synthetic¦passed");
    blk(&mut d, "row", "Field in sphere cells¦first-order better in every field¦synthetic¦passed");
    blk(&mut d, "row", "Grid in 3-D cells¦body-centred better in every field¦synthetic¦passed");
    attributed(&mut d, "row", "", "Grid in sphere cells¦geodesic better in every field¦synthetic¦passed", "last=1");
    labelled(&mut d, "section", "sec:glossary", "Glossary");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "Each term is defined where it is first used in the text. The same definitions, with the field each comes from and what each excludes, are collected here.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "{b:sampling lattice}. A regular arrangement of points at which a continuous signal is measured; square, hexagonal, cubic and body-centred cubic are examples, and each can represent signals up to a limit of fineness set by its geometry. Field: signal processing. Excludes irregular or adaptive sample placements, and the pixel shape of a display.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "{b:preregistration}. A dated written plan for a study, fixed and hash-locked before any result is seen: the question, the data, the statistic, the controls, the forecasts and the kill conditions. Field: research method (metascience). Excludes a plan revised after looking at the outcome, and a protocol written only for the record after the run.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "{b:verdict}. The pass or fail of one preregistered rule set against the run's numbers, read off by code. Field: research method (our usage). Excludes an interpretation written after reading the numbers.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "{b:aliasing}. The error made when a signal holds detail finer than the samples can represent, so that the fine detail is rebuilt as false coarse detail. Field: signal processing. Excludes noise in the measurement, and blur from the rebuilding filter.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "{b:body-centred cubic lattice}. The cubic lattice with one more point at the centre of every cube; the face-centred cubic lattice instead adds a point at the centre of every face, and each of the two is the reciprocal of the other. Field: crystallography and sampling theory. Excludes a claim about any material's crystal structure.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "{b:geodesic grid}. A set of points on a sphere made by subdividing the faces of an icosahedron and pushing the points out to the surface; each point has six near-equal neighbours except twelve that have five, so the layout is close to hexagonal everywhere. Field: geometry and climate modelling. Excludes a latitude-longitude grid, whose points crowd together at the poles.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "{b:PSNR}. Peak signal-to-noise ratio, a measure in decibels of how closely a rebuilt image matches the original; higher is closer, and 1 dB is a change of about 21 percent in mean squared error. Field: image processing. Excludes a measure of how good the image looks to a viewer.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "{b:frame condition number}. The ratio B/A of a frame's upper and lower bounds, which says how much rebuilding can amplify errors in the coefficients; 1 is ideal. Field: time-frequency analysis. Excludes a measure of how few coefficients a signal needs.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "{b:Gabor frame}. A set of copies of one short window, shifted in time and in frequency onto a lattice of points, such that any signal can be rebuilt stably from how strongly it matches each copy. Field: time-frequency analysis. Excludes a basis; a frame has more members than strictly needed, by a factor called the redundancy.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "{b:95% interval}. The range that holds the middle 95 percent of the bootstrap or control draws for a number; we report it beside the number. Field: statistics. Excludes a statement that the true value lies inside with 95 percent probability.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "{b:Benjamini-Hochberg correction}. An adjustment of a set of p values that holds the expected share of false discoveries among the results called significant at a chosen level. Field: statistics. Excludes a guarantee that no result in the set is a false discovery.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "{b:control}. A version of the data with the structure under test removed (for example shuffled or pooled), scored the same way as the real data, so the real score has something to beat. Field: statistics and experimental design. Excludes a comparison with a different dataset or with a published number.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "{b:planted check}. Data built so that the right answer is known, run through the same analysis before the real data, to show that the analysis can see the effect when it is there. Field: research method; called a positive control in experimental science. Excludes any check made on the real data, which cannot show what the analysis misses.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "{b:findings ledger}. The append-only, dated list of every result we have recorded, numbered F1, F2 and on; corrections are added as new dated lines and old entries are never rewritten. Field: our records. Excludes a curated list of successes.");
    blk(&mut d, "section", "References");
    labelled(&mut d, "bib", "petersen1962", "Petersen, D. P. and Middleton, D. (1962). Sampling and reconstruction of wave-number-limited functions in N-dimensional Euclidean spaces. Information and Control 5(4), 279-323.");
    labelled(&mut d, "bib", "entezari", "Entezari, A., Van De Ville, D. and Möller, T. Practical box splines for reconstruction on the body centered cubic lattice. IEEE Transactions on Visualization and Computer Graphics.");
    labelled(&mut d, "bib", "agrell", "Multidimensional sampling of isotropically bandlimited signals. arXiv:1703.00471");
    labelled(&mut d, "bib", "strohmer2003", "Strohmer, T. and Beaver, S. (2003). Optimal OFDM design for time-frequency dispersive channels. IEEE Transactions on Communications 51(7), 1111-1122. doi:10.1109/TCOMM.2003.814200");
    labelled(&mut d, "bib", "faulhuber2019", "Faulhuber, M. (2019). The Strohmer and Beaver conjecture for Gaussian Gabor systems: a deep mathematical problem (?). arXiv:1905.05051");
    labelled(&mut d, "bib", "kaushik2026", "Kaushik, Paul and Yuille (2026). Honeycomb-Surfels: compact hexagonal primitives for efficient scene rendering and reconstruction. arXiv:2610.37668");
    labelled(&mut d, "bib", "bh1995", "Benjamini, Y. and Hochberg, Y. (1995). Controlling the false discovery rate: a practical and powerful approach to multiple testing. Journal of the Royal Statistical Society, Series B 57, 289-300.");
    let ok = save(&d, "F109.doc.tsv");
    println!("{} blocks ({})", d.blocks, ok);
}
